1️⃣ Classic Generational Heap (Serial / Parallel / CMS-style)
========================================================================
Heap layout
Heap
 ├── Young Generation
 │    ├── Eden
 │    ├── Survivor S0
 │    └── Survivor S1
 └── Old / Tenured Generation

Object lifecycle
- New objects → Eden
- Minor GC:
    - Live objects copied Eden → S0/S1
    - Age incremented
When age ≥ -XX:MaxTenuringThreshold → promoted to Old Gen

2️⃣ GC Phases (Mark / Sweep / Compact)
========================================================================
Phase	Meaning
-------------------------
Mark	Find live objects
Sweep	Reclaim dead objects
Compact	Defragment heap
-------------------------

⚠️ Note:
- Sweep ≠ Delete individual objects
    It reclaims regions of dead memory
- Compaction may or may not happen depending on collector

3️⃣ Minor vs Major GC
------------------------------------------------
GC Type 	Scope	            STW
------------------------------------------------
Minor GC	Young Gen only	    Yes
Major GC	Old Gen	Usually     Yes
Full GC	    Entire Heap	        Yes (long)
------------------------------------------------

When old generation is full ⇒ Major / Full GC
✔️ Yes (especially for Serial / Parallel)

4️⃣ Stop-The-World (STW)
Threads paused -> GC runs -> Resume
But duration differs massively by collector.

5️⃣ Collector Types — Corrections & Clarifications
========================================================================
🧵 Serial GC ->Single-threaded -> STW for everything -> Small heaps / dev / embedded
⚡ Parallel GC (Throughput GC) -> Multi-threaded GC -> Still STW -> Optimized for throughput, not latency
🧠 Concurrent Collectors (CMS / G1 / ZGC / Shenandoah)
    >> Concurrent collectors do most work concurrently, but still require short STW phases (initial mark, remark, etc.)

6️⃣ G1GC — This Is Where the Model Changes 🔥
========================================================================
❌ What no longer exists physically

In G1GC, the heap is NOT physically divided like this:

❌ Young Gen (Eden + S0 + S1)
❌ Old Gen

That layout does NOT exist as fixed memory areas.

✅ G1GC Heap Model
Heap
 ├── Region 1
 ├── Region 2
 ├── Region 3
 ├── ...
 ├── Region N


Heap split into equal-sized regions (1–32 MB)
Each region can dynamically be:
    Eden
    Survivor
    Old
    Humongous

📌 Role is logical, not physical
Young Gen in G1GC (Logical, not fixed)
At any moment:
==================================================
Young Gen = { Eden regions + Survivor regions }
Old Gen   = { Old regions }
================================================

➡️ But:
These are just labels
Regions can change roles between cycles


7️⃣ G1GC Collection Behavior (Your Best Part 💯)

It divides heap into different regions and during a GC it can collect a subset of regions

✔️ 100% correct

Regions with most garbage will be collected first

✔️ Exactly — hence the name Garbage First

How G1 decides what to collect
 - Tracks live-data density per region
 - Builds a collection set (CSet)
 - Picks regions with:
    - High garbage
    - Within pause-time goal (-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis)

8️⃣ Major GC in G1GC (Important Correction)

When old generation full ⇒ Major GC

❌ Not strictly true for G1

In G1:
There is no “old-gen full → stop the world” rule
G1 performs:
 - Mixed GC (Young + some Old regions)
 - Concurrent marking
Full GC is last resort
✔️ G1 tries very hard to avoid Full GC

